nodecode-reinfolib is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-reinfolib:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-reinfolib
  commit cfb17760af4b44df2057a262c16f90dfef5066ee
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole plugin in Common Lisp, in one file. The fetch is the same
one GET to /ex-api/external/XIT001 with the same five parameters, the key in the
same Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key header and never in a URL, the same eight-megabyte
body cap, the same one-second gap between requests, the same refusal of a plain
http origin that is not loopback, and the same scan of the answer for the key's
text. The projection is the plugin's twelve columns, the twenty-row cap, the
digits-only TradePrice median with its .5 half, the counts and medians by type,
and MLIT's attribution, processing note and terms sentence verbatim. The argument
rules are the plugin's, in its order and its words: a five-digit code whose
prefix is 01-47, the designated-city codes refused with a ward-code example, 2005
from quarter 3, a quarter that has not started refused, and the full-width digits
a Japanese keyboard produces folded to ASCII. The tool keeps its name
(reinfolib_quarter), and the slash command and the skill's procedure are the
command and (help :reinfolib).

Left out, because they are Hermes surfaces rather than the read: the bundled
skill's tool_search/tool_call indirection (this model calls the tool directly),
the Automation Blueprint that schedules the weekly check (the cron cell schedules
in Nodecode), and Hermes's [SILENT] delivery rule for a week with no data, which
belongs to a scheduler. The JSON payload the plugin returned is rendered as the
text a Nodecode tool answers with, carrying the same facts. The rewrite follows
redirects with the shared HTTP client, up to five, where the plugin refused any
3xx, so a redirect is only reported when the last hop is one. A key leak is
detected in the answer's text after one percent or \u decoding, where the plugin
also scanned a JSON re-encoding of the payload.
